  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 AM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nPrecipitation has ended across the area, with winds finally \nsubsiding over northern Johnson County. Skies remain overcast over \nmuch of the CWA early this morning, with clear skies over \nsouthwestern portions of the state. Low clouds/fog will be possible \nat times through the rest of the morning, with many locations having \ncloud heights drop below 1000ft at times already. There is not a \nstrong signal for fog with low visibilities at any particular area, \nbut will continue to keep an eye on any developing trends. \nSkies will quickly scatter out by 18Z, with mostly clear skies \nacross areas east of the Divide. \n\nThere are not many changes with the forecast from the previous \ndiscussion. Conditions are expected to remain dry through the rest \nof the week, as an area of high pressure settles over MT today. \nTemperatures will stay at or slightly below normal through the week \nwith winds staying light. The high center will also keep a weak \nPacific system to the south over UT/CO Wednesday, with the main \nimpact being mainly in the form of cloudy skies over \nsouthern/southwestern portions. Isolated showers will be possible \nover the western half of Sweetwater County and southern Lincoln \nCounty from the northern end of this low pressure system. \n\nThe pattern then briefly becomes more active by Thursday as a trough \nmoves over the Northern Rockies and pushes the high center over the \nNorthern Plains. The chance for high winds in northern Johnson \nCounty no longer looks likely as models have now trended toward a \nridge building over the Great Basin and Northern Rockies, keeping \nthe PFJ further north away from the region.  Temperatures will trend \nabove normal Sunday and Monday as the aforementioned ridge shifts \nmore over the Northern Rockies and Alberta/Saskatchewan.",
